1
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:07,160
On a quiet stretch of the Rhine, we kept seeing them: small dimples on the water,

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,390
drifting beside the boat.

3
00:00:12,490 --> 00:00:15,940
Some lasted a surprisingly long time.

4
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:19,190
Each one marks a whirlpool.

5
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:28,320
Spinning water needs an inward push, and pressure provides it: higher outside, lower toward the centre.

6
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:32,580
So above the low-pressure core, the surface dips.

7
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:36,010
But a river is restless.

8
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:39,690
Everything in it gets sheared and stirred.

9
00:00:39,730 --> 00:00:43,340
So what keeps one tiny whirlpool going?

10
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:50,600
Underneath is a narrow tube of spinning water, reaching down into the river.

11
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:55,610
Draw a line along its middle: a vortex line.

12
00:00:55,650 --> 00:01:00,850
The water turns around it; the line is how we draw the spin.

13
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:06,970
In 1858, Hermann von Helmholtz described a rule:

14
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:13,081
an isolated tube of spin cannot simply have a loose end inside the water.

15
00:01:13,631 --> 00:01:16,171
Draw a loop around it.

16
00:01:16,211 --> 00:01:23,541
Along each little piece, take the water's speed in that direction, times the piece's length.

17
00:01:23,581 --> 00:01:27,511
Add those contributions: circulation.

18
00:01:28,061 --> 00:01:30,071
Freeze the flow.

19
00:01:30,111 --> 00:01:35,551
Imagine the tube ended, and slide our measuring loop past that end,

20
00:01:35,591 --> 00:01:38,991
without crossing any spinning water.

21
00:01:39,031 --> 00:01:42,541
Its circulation should stay the same.

22
00:01:42,581 --> 00:01:48,171
But now we can shrink the loop to a point: zero circulation.

23
00:01:48,211 --> 00:01:51,591
That contradiction rules out the loose end.

24
00:01:53,741 --> 00:01:57,351
A closed ring has no loose end.

25
00:01:57,391 --> 00:02:00,781
A tube can also connect to the surface.

26
00:02:02,131 --> 00:02:07,671
A smoke ring is that closed shape: a tube of spinning air.

27
00:02:07,711 --> 00:02:10,451
The smoke lets us see it roll.

28
00:02:12,001 --> 00:02:20,721
In 1867, in Edinburgh, Peter Guthrie Tait showed his smoke rings to his friend William Thomson,

29
00:02:20,761 --> 00:02:23,311
later Lord Kelvin.

30
00:02:23,861 --> 00:02:28,171
The rings bounced and wobbled without falling apart.

31
00:02:28,211 --> 00:02:34,431
Under the right conditions, two rings can even take turns passing through each other.

32
00:02:35,981 --> 00:02:40,641
Kelvin saw a possible answer to a much bigger question.

33
00:02:40,681 --> 00:02:46,881
In a perfect, frictionless fluid, a ring's circulation would never fade.

34
00:02:46,921 --> 00:02:52,951
So maybe atoms were vortex rings in the ether, and each element a different knot.

35
00:02:53,501 --> 00:02:56,401
Kelvin was wrong about atoms.

36
00:02:56,441 --> 00:03:01,611
But Tait's attempts to catalogue the knots helped build knot theory.

37
00:03:03,161 --> 00:03:07,371
Now bring a tilted ring up under the water's surface.

38
00:03:07,411 --> 00:03:11,721
Its upper edge arrives first, and it can reconnect,

39
00:03:11,761 --> 00:03:15,431
leaving a curved tube with two ends at the surface.

40
00:03:15,981 --> 00:03:19,071
From above, two dimples.

41
00:03:19,111 --> 00:03:22,361
From below, one connected tube.

42
00:03:25,911 --> 00:03:30,281
Its spin points up at one end and down at the other.

43
00:03:30,321 --> 00:03:34,281
From above, the ends turn opposite ways.

44
00:03:35,631 --> 00:03:39,181
The Rhine doesn't need a smoke box.

45
00:03:39,221 --> 00:03:42,751
The current makes spinning structures for itself.

46
00:03:43,301 --> 00:03:46,111
One source is the riverbed.

47
00:03:46,151 --> 00:03:52,691
Friction slows the water nearest the bottom, while the water above keeps moving.

48
00:03:52,731 --> 00:03:57,901
That shear feeds vortices, including arches called hairpins.

49
00:03:58,451 --> 00:04:02,421
These structures can be stretched and dispersed.

50
00:04:02,461 --> 00:04:05,851
Some can reach the surface and connect to it.

51
00:04:06,401 --> 00:04:11,831
In our ideal pair, each whirlpool rides the other's swirl.

52
00:04:11,871 --> 00:04:17,621
Bring them closer, keeping their strengths fixed, and the pair moves faster.

53
00:04:19,371 --> 00:04:22,231
Obstacles make wakes too.

54
00:04:22,271 --> 00:04:29,941
Under suitable conditions, a rock or a bridge pier can shed alternating whirlpools downstream.

55
00:04:30,491 --> 00:04:33,651
In this photograph of a Norwegian river,

56
00:04:33,691 --> 00:04:39,911
the researchers identify the largest dimples as vortices shed by a bridge pillar.

57
00:04:40,461 --> 00:04:47,091
Experiments show that changing the depth over an obstacle can change its wake dramatically.

58
00:04:48,441 --> 00:04:51,441
So what keeps a whirlpool going?

59
00:04:51,991 --> 00:04:57,481
Left alone, friction spreads its spin into the water around it.

60
00:04:57,521 --> 00:05:01,441
The core widens, and the dimple gets shallower.

61
00:05:01,991 --> 00:05:05,691
But the river can also work the other way.

62
00:05:05,731 --> 00:05:14,211
Where surface water converges and sinks, the downward flow stretches an upright whirlpool and spins it faster.

63
00:05:14,251 --> 00:05:18,141
The surrounding surface can stay nearly level.

64
00:05:18,181 --> 00:05:22,421
That doesn't mean the water underneath only moves sideways.

65
00:05:22,971 --> 00:05:27,961
And a dimple can vanish from view before its whirlpool does.

66
00:05:28,001 --> 00:05:33,141
Ripples can hide the dimple without destroying the whirlpool underneath.

67
00:05:33,691 --> 00:05:37,561
A shallow dip bends the reflection above it.

68
00:05:37,601 --> 00:05:43,251
Keep the whirlpool unchanged, but move a cloud's reflection across it.

69
00:05:43,291 --> 00:05:45,471
Now the dimple shows.

70
00:05:47,021 --> 00:05:52,671
Smooth water, distinct reflections, and a low viewing angle help.

71
00:05:52,711 --> 00:05:55,691
That day, we had all three.

72
00:05:57,041 --> 00:06:02,361
Those hidden motions are what researchers are learning to read from the surface.

73
00:06:02,401 --> 00:06:07,891
A team in Trondheim, including Omer Babiker and Simen Ellingsen,

74
00:06:07,931 --> 00:06:15,611
found in simulations that regional dimple counts tracked how strongly the surface was spreading and converging.

75
00:06:16,161 --> 00:06:21,361
In 2026, laboratory experiments supported the link,

76
00:06:21,401 --> 00:06:28,661
this time measuring the area covered by dimples and by elongated marks called scars.

77
00:06:29,211 --> 00:06:32,091
That churning renews the surface,

78
00:06:32,131 --> 00:06:39,531
and helps set how fast gases like oxygen and carbon dioxide cross between water and air.

79
00:06:40,081 --> 00:06:44,481
We couldn't find a published count of them on any real river.

80
00:06:45,031 --> 00:06:50,621
On a sunny day, look from a bridge into clear, shallow water.

81
00:06:50,661 --> 00:06:55,881
A dimple can bend sunlight into a dark patch with a bright rim.

82
00:06:56,431 --> 00:07:00,651
A dimple isn't a map of everything underneath.

83
00:07:00,691 --> 00:07:04,661
But those little dents are clues to the river below.
